LP.section({"id": "industries", "title": "Industries & Company Types", "icon": "🏢", "blurb": "Product vs service vs FinTech & more — where data/AI engineers work and how to choose."});
LP.page({"id": "industry-landscape", "section": "industries", "title": "The tech landscape — who hires data & AI engineers", "level": "Zero", "minutes": 9, "tags": ["industries", "careers", "must-know"], "body": "\nWhen people say \"I work in tech,\" they could mean wildly different jobs at wildly different *kinds* of companies. Two engineers with the same title — \"Data Engineer\" — can have completely different days depending on the **type of company** they work for. Understanding these types early helps you pick a first job, read a job description correctly, and answer the interview question *\"why do you want to work here?\"* convincingly.\n\n<div class=\"eli\"><span class=\"eli-tag\">Explain like I'm new<\/span>\nA \"company type\" is just <em>how a company makes money and who it sells to<\/em>. A restaurant that cooks its own signature dishes (a <strong>product<\/strong>) is different from a catering company that cooks whatever each client orders (a <strong>service<\/strong>). Same skill — cooking — very different day.\n<\/div>\n\n## The three big buckets you'll hear about\n\n| Type | How it makes money | Classic examples |\n|------|--------------------|------------------|\n| **Product-based** | Builds its *own* product/platform and sells it to many users | Google, Netflix, Flipkart, a SaaS startup |\n| **Service-based** | Builds/maintains software *for other companies* (clients) | Infosys, TCS, Accenture, Capgemini |\n| **Domain-based** (e.g. FinTech, HealthTech) | A product *or* service company focused on one industry | Razorpay (FinTech), a hospital-analytics firm (HealthTech) |\n\nThe key insight beginners miss: **these buckets overlap.** \"FinTech\" isn't the *opposite* of \"product-based\" — a FinTech like Stripe **is** a product company that happens to work in finance. \"Domain\" describes *what field* you're in; \"product vs service\" describes *how the company operates*. A company sits in both at once.\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-note\"><span class=\"cfor\"><\/span><div><span class=\"ctitle\">Two questions, not one:<\/span> For any company ask (1) <em>Product or service?<\/em> (how they operate) and (2) <em>What domain?<\/em> (finance, retail, health, generic). Razorpay = product + FinTech. Infosys building a bank's app = service + FinTech-adjacent. Netflix = product + media/streaming.<\/div><\/div>\n\n## Why this matters for *data & AI* work specifically\n\nData and AI roles exist in **all** of these, but the work differs:\n\n- **Product company** → you own data pipelines/ML for *one* evolving product. You go **deep**: the same domain, same data, for years. You see your features reach millions of users.\n- **Service company** → you build data solutions for *many* clients across industries. You go **broad**: new domain, new stack every project. Excellent for learning fast early in your career.\n- **Domain company (FinTech/HealthTech…)** → the *domain rules* shape everything: compliance, accuracy, latency, privacy. A FinTech data engineer thinks about fraud, reconciliation, and audit trails daily.\n\n<div class=\"pro\"><span class=\"pro-tag\">Going deeper · for the experienced<\/span>\nThe product/service split also predicts your <strong>engineering maturity exposure<\/strong>. Product companies tend to invest in platform, internal tooling, and long-lived data contracts (you'll meet feature stores, data mesh, SLAs on datasets). Classic service engagements optimise for delivery against a client SOW, so you may rotate stacks and inherit legacy — great for breadth, but you must be deliberate about depth. Many seniors deliberately zig-zag: service first for breadth, product later for depth and ownership.\n<\/div>\n\n## A quick map of domains you'll encounter\n\n- **FinTech** — payments, lending, trading, banking tech (Razorpay, Stripe, Zerodha).\n- **E-commerce / Retail** — catalogs, recommendations, logistics (Flipkart, Amazon, Walmart).\n- **HealthTech** — patient data, diagnostics, claims (privacy-critical).\n- **EdTech** — learning platforms, content, assessment.\n- **AdTech / MarTech** — huge real-time event data, targeting.\n- **Media / Streaming** — recommendations at scale (Netflix, Spotify).\n- **Enterprise SaaS** — software other businesses run on (Salesforce, SAP).\n- **GovTech / Consulting** — large, often service-led, compliance-heavy.\n\nEach domain is a *flavour* layered on top of the product/service way of working.\n\n> **Key takeaway:** Describe any company with **two** dimensions — *how it operates* (product vs service) and *what domain it serves* (FinTech, retail, health…). They overlap; they're not rivals. The next lessons compare them in depth and help you choose.\n"});
LP.page({"id": "product-service-fintech", "section": "industries", "title": "Product vs Service vs FinTech — compared", "level": "Beginner", "minutes": 11, "tags": ["industries", "product", "service", "fintech", "careers"], "body": "\nLet's put the three side by side — what the day actually looks like, what they pay for, and what they want from *you*. This is the lesson to re-read before any interview where someone asks **\"why this company?\"**\n\n## At a glance\n\n<div class=\"cmp-grid\">\n<div class=\"cmp-card\"><h4>Product-based<\/h4><span class=\"cmp-tag\">Builds & owns one product<\/span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Depth<\/strong> in one domain & codebase<\/li>\n<li>Long-lived data, real ownership<\/li>\n<li>Higher bar to enter, higher ceiling<\/li>\n<li>Pay: often highest (equity/RSUs)<\/li>\n<li>e.g. Google, Netflix, Flipkart, SaaS startups<\/li>\n<\/ul><\/div>\n<div class=\"cmp-card\"><h4>Service-based<\/h4><span class=\"cmp-tag\">Builds for many clients<\/span>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breadth<\/strong> across domains & stacks<\/li>\n<li>Fast learning, lots of mentorship/training<\/li>\n<li>Easier entry — great first job<\/li>\n<li>Pay: steady; grows with skill & switches<\/li>\n<li>e.g. Infosys, TCS, Accenture, Capgemini, Wipro<\/li>\n<\/ul><\/div>\n<div class=\"cmp-card\"><h4>FinTech (a domain)<\/h4><span class=\"cmp-tag\">Finance, as product or service<\/span>\n<ul>\n<li>Domain rules dominate: accuracy, audit, latency<\/li>\n<li>Heavy compliance & security<\/li>\n<li>Data correctness is non-negotiable<\/li>\n<li>Pay: strong; values domain knowledge<\/li>\n<li>e.g. Razorpay, Stripe, Zerodha, bank tech arms<\/li>\n<\/ul><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-warn\"><span class=\"cfor\"><\/span><div><span class=\"ctitle\">Don't compare apples to oranges:<\/span> Product vs service is a real either/or. FinTech is <em>not<\/em> a third option on that axis — it's a <em>domain<\/em> that can be product (Stripe) or service (a consultancy building bank software). We list it here because beginners constantly ask \"product, service, or FinTech?\" — the honest answer is \"FinTech is product <em>or<\/em> service, plus finance rules.\"<\/div><\/div>\n\n## A day in the life\n\n**Product data engineer (say, at a streaming app):** You own the pipeline that turns raw \"play / pause / skip\" events into the tables the recommendation model trains on. You care about data freshness SLAs, schema contracts with other teams, and the cost of your daily Spark jobs. The domain (streaming) barely changes year to year, so you go *deep* — you become the person who knows that data cold.\n\n**Service data engineer (say, at Infosys on a retail client):** This quarter you're building a sales-analytics warehouse for a retailer; next year it might be a healthcare data lake. You ramp on a new domain and stack quickly, follow the client's standards, and deliver against a statement of work. You meet *many* architectures fast — unbeatable for early-career breadth.\n\n**FinTech data engineer (say, at a payments company):** Every pipeline you build must reconcile to the last paisa, keep an audit trail, and never lose a transaction. You think about idempotency, exactly-once processing, fraud signals, and regulatory reporting. The *domain* sets the bar — \"roughly right\" is never acceptable with money.\n\n<div class=\"pro\"><span class=\"pro-tag\">Going deeper · for the experienced<\/span>\nThe deciding technical variable is often <strong>data criticality<\/strong>. In media/retail, eventual consistency and approximate metrics are usually fine (a view count off by 0.1% is harmless). In FinTech/HealthTech, you're closer to OLTP-grade guarantees on analytical paths: exactly-once semantics, immutable audit logs, reconciliation jobs, PII handling (tokenisation, encryption at rest/in transit), and lineage you can show an auditor. This is why FinTech interviews probe ACID, idempotency, and CAP trade-offs harder — revisit those Core Concepts lessons before applying.\n<\/div>\n\n## Which should *you* target first?\n\nThere's no single right answer — match it to your goal:\n\n- **Brand-new, want to learn fast & get hired** → service-based is a superb on-ramp: structured training, many projects, lower entry bar.\n- **Want deep ownership & top pay, ready to grind interviews** → product-based.\n- **Love a field (money, health, games)** → pick that domain; decide product vs service by how you like to work.\n- **Unsure** → many careers go *service → product*: build breadth and a track record, then specialise.\n\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><span class=\"cfor\"><\/span><div><span class=\"ctitle\">Interview gold:<\/span> When asked \"why us?\", name the company's <em>type<\/em> and <em>domain<\/em> back to them. \"I want product depth in streaming data\" or \"I'm drawn to FinTech because correctness and audit trails make the data work rigorous\" lands far better than \"you're a great company.\"<\/div><\/div>\n\n> **Key takeaway:** Service = breadth & fast entry. Product = depth, ownership, higher ceiling. FinTech (and other domains) = a rigor-and-compliance flavour layered on either. Choose by how you like to work *and* what you like to work on.\n"});
